course: biology
lesson: 156
title: The Amazon canopy: howlers, harpy eagles, toucans, dart frogs and sloth gardens
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: High above the Amazon floor the tree tops join into a green roof called the canopy, full of sunlight, fruit, flowers and animals that may never touch the ground.
explain: At dawn howler monkeys roar so loudly they can be heard kilometres away, toucans flick berries into their throats, and a harpy eagle with claws as long as a bear's watches for a sloth.
explain: A sloth is a slow little garden: green algae grow in its fur, moths live there too, and once a week it climbs all the way down to poo, the riskiest trip of its life.
explain: Cartoons show sloths as lazy jokers and toucans as cereal-box clowns; the real reasons are better: leaves give a sloth very little energy, and a toucan's huge beak is light, spongy inside and works like a radiator.
fact: Howler monkeys roar together to tell other troops where they are, and a hollow bone in the throat lets the sound carry several kilometres.
fact: Howlers rest most of the day, because their leafy food gives little energy.
fact: The harpy eagle is one of the most powerful eagles on Earth; it hunts sloths and monkeys with back claws as long as a bear's, flying fast between branches on short broad wings.
fact: A toucan picks fruit with the tip of its beak and flicks it back into its throat; the beak is a light honeycomb of bone covered in keratin, the stuff of our nails, and also lets out body heat.
fact: Some poison dart frogs carry each tadpole on their back up to a little pool of water in a bromeliad plant, where there are no fish to eat it.
fact: Poison dart frogs wear bright colours as a warning; wild frogs get their poison from the ants and mites they eat, so zoo frogs on other food are not poisonous.
fact: Three-toed sloths come down to the ground about once a week to poo, and algae and moths live in their fur.
fact: Macaws flock to clay licks and nibble soil, which gives them scarce salt and may soak up bitter poisons from unripe seeds.
fact: A spider monkey's tail grips a branch like a fifth hand, with ridged bare skin on the tip like a fingerprint.
fact: Only monkeys of the Americas have gripping tails; no monkey of Africa or Asia can hang by its tail.
fact: Epiphytes such as orchids, ferns and bromeliads live on branches high up, catching rain and fallen bits instead of rooting in the soil.
fact: When researchers sprayed a harmless mist into single canopy trees, beetles of hundreds of kinds fell out, many never named.
fact: Squirrel monkeys travel in big troops and often follow capuchin monkeys to find fruit trees, and many eyes spot eagles sooner.
exercise: Why do howler monkeys roar at dawn? | To tell other troops where they are | Because they are hungry and want more food | To scare off the rain | To wake up the sloths
exercise: What lets a howler's roar carry so far? | A big hollow bone in its throat | Its huge ears | Its long tail | Its open mouth only
exercise: Why do howlers rest most of the day? | Their leafy food gives little energy | They are always ill | They roar all night | They are scared of the hot sun in the treetops
exercise: What does a harpy eagle hunt in the canopy? | Sloths and monkeys | Fish and frogs only | Fruit and nuts from the trees | Grass and fallen leaves
exercise: Why does a harpy eagle have short broad wings? | To fly fast between branches in the forest | To soar high over the open sea, looking for fish | To swim underwater | To dig its nest
exercise: How does a toucan eat a berry? | It picks it with its beak tip and flicks it back into its throat | It chews it with its teeth | It squashes it with its feet | It drinks only the sweet juice of the fruit, sucking it up through its hollow beak
exercise: Why is a toucan's huge beak not too heavy? | It is a spongy honeycomb of bone inside | It is hollow and full of air like a balloon | It is made of feathers packed tightly together | It is made of soft rubber, like a bouncy ball
exercise: How does a toucan's beak help on a hot day? | It lets out body heat like a radiator | It fans cool air onto its face when it flies | It stores cold water to drink on hot days | It shades its eyes from the bright sun
exercise: Where does a dart frog carry its tadpole? | Up to a little pool of water in a bromeliad | Down to the big river | Into a hole in the ground | Into a bird's nest
exercise: Why does a poison dart frog sit in the open in bright colours? | The colours warn that its skin is poisonous | It is lost and wants another frog to come and find it | It is too slow to hide | It is warming up in the sun
exercise: Where do wild dart frogs get their poison? | From the ants and mites they eat | They make it from sunlight | From the water they sit in | From the leaves they sit on
exercise: How often does a three-toed sloth climb down to poo? | About once a week | About every hour or so | Only about once a year | Never, it poos from the branch
exercise: What grows in a sloth's fur? | Green algae, with moths living there too | Moss and tiny mushrooms, but nothing else | Flowers and little fruits that birds come to eat | Nothing at all, its fur is always perfectly clean
exercise: Why do macaws eat clay from river cliffs? | It gives them salt and may soak up poisons from seeds | They like the crunchy taste | They are using it to build strong mud nests for their eggs | They are cleaning their beaks
exercise: How does a spider monkey use its tail? | As a fifth hand to grip branches | Only for balance like a cat | To swat flies away | To dig for roots
exercise: What are epiphytes? | Plants like orchids and ferns that grow on trees without roots in the soil | Plants that eat insects | Plants that grow underwater | Plants that live only on the ground, in the deep shade under the trees, with long roots
exercise: What did researchers find when they misted one canopy tree? | Hundreds of kinds of beetles, many never named | Only one kind of beetle | No insects at all | Mostly birds and bats, with hardly any insects
exercise: Why do squirrel monkeys follow capuchins? | Capuchins know where fruit trees are | Capuchins carry them on their backs | They want to steal capuchin babies | They are lost
test: A cartoon sloth is so lazy it falls asleep mid-sentence. What is the real reason sloths are slow? | Their leafy food gives very little energy, so moving slowly saves it | They are just lazy | They are always ill | They want to look funny and harmless so that jaguars and eagles leave them alone
test: «sloth's coming down the tree, he's gonna have a wee walk» What is it really doing? | Climbing down to poo, about once a week | Looking for a new tree to live in, as they move every night | Going for a swim every day | Hunting for insects
test: A spider monkey hangs by its tail and picks fruit with both hands. How many hands and tails is it using? | 2 hands and 1 tail | 1 hand and 2 tails | 4 hands and 1 tail | 2 hands and no tail
test: A troop of 4 spider monkeys hangs from a branch, each by its tail only. How many tails are holding on? | 4 | 8 | 16 | 2
test: «zoo dart frog's poisonous, don't touch it!» What is right? | Zoo frogs fed other food are not poisonous, though you still should not grab them | Yes, zoo frogs are the most poisonous of all, because they are fed so well every day | Yes, they make poison from sunlight | No dart frog is ever poisonous
test: «I saw a monkey hang by its tail in Africa» What would you say? | Probably not; only monkeys of the Americas have gripping tails | Yes, all monkeys do that | Yes, but only monkeys from Africa and Asia can hang by their tails | Monkeys have no tails
test: What would happen if harpy eagles vanished from a forest? | There could be more sloths and monkeys eating the leaves and fruit | The forest would lose all its trees at once and turn into a desert | The howlers would stop roaring | Nothing at all would change
test: «toucan's beak is a giant banana, they eat with their feet» What is right? | The beak is light bone inside, and it flicks fruit into its throat | Yes, the beak is filled with soft fruit like a bag it carries around | Yes, toucans eat with their feet | The beak is made of hard wood
test: A whole bunch of macaws are licking a muddy cliff. Why? | The clay gives them salt and may soak up poisons from seeds | They are drinking rain water | They are polishing their beaks | They are lost and hungry for mud only
test: Orchids grow on a branch high above the ground. How do they get water? | Their roots and leaves catch rain and fallen bits | Long roots grow all the way down the trunk to reach the soil below | They suck water from the tree like a vampire | They do not need water
test: «only a few kinds of beetle, beetles are boring» What did canopy research show? | A single tree can hold hundreds of kinds of beetles, many unnamed | Each tree has one beetle | All beetles live on the ground | Beetles do not live in rainforests
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocalisations of the howler monkey. | They are produced collectively to inform neighbouring troops of the group's position | They are produced exclusively in response to hunger | They are inaudible beyond a distance of a few metres, being produced very softly and only to the mother | They are produced solely during the hours of darkness
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the three-toed sloth descends from the canopy approximately once each week. | To defecate upon the ground | To drink from the river below the tree | To seek a mate upon the forest floor | To sleep within a burrow in the earth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the source of the poison of wild poison dart frogs? | It is derived from the ants and mites consumed by the frogs | It is produced from the absorption of sunlight | It is absorbed from the water of the bromeliad | It is acquired from the leaves upon which the frogs rest, and soaked up through the skin
exam: Ascertain the function of the gripping tail of the spider monkey. | It supports the full weight of the animal in the manner of a fifth hand | It serves exclusively for balance during leaping | It serves to repel insects | It is employed principally in the excavation of roots and in the digging of nests
